    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f5407c8c-f10f-5f5f-8794-8d18d4ed8237",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply. A vector field maps points of a subset of $\\mathbb R^2$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$; a restricted natural domain is allowed.\n\n- **I.** $B(x,y)=(x^2)\\,\\mathbf i+(y+0)\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- **II.** $P(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i+(y+0)\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- **III.** $Q(x,y)=\\langle \\sqrt{x},y+0,2\\rangle$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Check the input dimension and output dimension.\n\n- **I.** $B$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 2 components. All components are real on $\\mathbb R^2$. Both dimensions are two, so select this function.\n\n- **II.** $P$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 2 components. Its natural real domain is $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2:x\\ne 0\\}$, which is nonempty. A restricted natural domain is allowed. Both dimensions are two, so select this function.\n\n- **III.** $Q$ takes 2 input coordinates and returns a vector with 3 components. Its natural real domain is $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2:x\\geq 0\\}$, which is nonempty. A restricted natural domain is allowed. The input and output are not both two-dimensional, so do not select this function.\n\nSelect I, II only.",
